{"id":"n-20261005-qdnwzk","category":"travel","display_title":"Exploring Empty Spain","display_title_he":"חקר ספרד הריקה","short_summary":"An exploration of Spain's sparsely populated interior regions, highlighting astrotourism, recovering wildlife populations, local gastronomy, and rural tranquility.","english_level":"C1","words":507,"reading_time":3,"image":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Salvador_Dali_A_%28Dali_Atomicus%29_09633u.jpg/960px-Salvador_Dali_A_%28Dali_Atomicus%29_09633u.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Philippe Halsman · Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Salvador_Dali_A_(Dali_Atomicus)_09633u.jpg"},"sources":[{"name":"The Guardian","domain":"theguardian.com","url":"https://www.theguardian.com/travel/2026/oct/04/empty-spain-extremadura-castile-and-leon-wildlife-holiday","title":"Alone with the stars, lynx and wolves in ‘Empty Spain’","published_at":"2026-10-04T06:00:20.000Z"}],"published_at":"2026-10-04T06:00:20.000Z","first_published_in_app_at":"2026-10-05T20:53:13.364Z","quality_score":9,"interest_score":8,"verification_status":"verified","content_status":"published","paragraphs":["While bustling coastal resorts and major cities like Madrid and Barcelona grapple with heavy tourism, Spain’s vast interior regions tell a remarkably different story. Spanning much of what locals call La España Vacía, or “Empty Spain,” the adjoining territories of Castile and León and Extremadura remain largely untouched by mass tourism. Satellite imagery reveals these areas as dark voids bordered by illuminated coasts, a characteristic that now offers an unexpected advantage for travelers seeking unpolluted skies, lower prices, and profound solitude.","Under the auspices of an astrotourism initiative known as Extremadura Buenas Noches, visitors can follow curated stargazing routes across remote landscapes. These itineraries lead to secluded historical landmarks such as La Siberia and the Corral de los Lobos, an ancient stone structure once used to trap wolves. On clear nights, stargazers can observe celestial wonders ranging from the International Space Station to distant planets and the Milky Way. Forestry engineer and nature guide Alberto Garzón Fernández notes that the complete absence of light and industrial pollution makes the area exceptionally pristine.","Beyond pristine night skies, the region’s minimalist human presence has fostered a remarkable resurgence of vulnerable wildlife. According to local conservation guides, sound management has allowed populations of the Spanish imperial eagle to thrive. Meanwhile, the endangered Iberian lynx is steadily recovering within Monfragüe national park, and the Iberian wolf has returned to the flanking Gredos mountains, allowing hikers to hear their howls once again. These signature species represent a hopeful milestone for regional conservation efforts, proving that proactive environmental protection can yield tangible results.","Nevertheless, the region is not entirely immune to contemporary environmental hazards. Severe wildfires during the summer of 2025 ravaged nearly 20,000 hectares of the Jerte valley's renowned cherry orchards, threatening upcoming agricultural harvests. In nearby mountain villages such as Valverde de la Vera and La Alberca, depopulation remains a persistent challenge. Historic streets lined with medieval architecture and ruined castles often display a gothic-romantic hush, offering visitors plenty of quietude while presenting ongoing economic hurdles for local small businesses and grocery store owners.","For accommodation, travelers frequently utilize Spain’s state-owned parador network, which converts historical convents, castles, and palaces into affordable lodging within cities like Cáceres, Trujillo, and Plasencia. These urban centers serve as ideal bases for venturing into the surrounding countryside, where local traditions remain deeply rooted. Fun facts about the area include a local population of acorn-fed Iberian pigs that outnumbers human residents, famously roaming the dehesa forests before producing world-renowned jamón ibérico.","Gastronomy in Empty Spain offers exceptional value and quality, often surpassing the culinary experiences found in busier, more commercialized parts of the country. Biologist and guide Marino Esteban highlights the rich biodiversity of the local oak forests, which yield abundant wild mushrooms, chestnuts, and herbs. At rustic taverns like Meson de San Martín, visitors can savor freshly grilled penny bun mushrooms, local cheeses, and robust red wines produced from native rufete grapes, all at a fraction of the cost typical of prominent wine regions like La Rioja."],"facts":[{"fact":"Castile and León and Extremadura make up a large part of what is known as 'Empty Spain' (La España Vacía), characterized by a sparse population and minimal light pollution.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Extremadura Buenas Noches is an astrotourism initiative guiding visitors through optimal stargazing routes.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Vulnerable native wildlife including the Iberian lynx, Iberian wolf, and Spanish imperial eagle are making a comeback in the region due to conservation and sound management.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Heatwave-borne wildfires in 2025 burned nearly 20,000 hectares of the Jerte valley's famous cherry orchards.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Visitors to Empty Spain can enjoy affordable, high-quality local gastronomy, including jamón ibérico, native wines, mushrooms, and local produce.","sources":[1]}],"glossary":[{"text":"Spain","lemma":"Spain","pos":"name","he":"ספרד","name":true},{"text":"Castile and León","lemma":"Castile and León","pos":"name","he":"קסטיליה ולאון","name":true},{"text":"Extremadura","lemma":"Extremadura","pos":"name","he":"אסטרמדורה","name":true},{"text":"Madrid","lemma":"Madrid","pos":"name","he":"מדריד","name":true},{"text":"Barcelona","lemma":"Barce